# Kindness and Firmness Are Needed!
Tamil title: கனிவும் கண்டிப்பும் தேவை!

Summary of the speech delivered by Arignar Anna at a felicitation meeting for Madras District D.M.K. volunteers on 6-11-1960:

On this platform now, the Mayor is seated on one side and the Deputy Mayor on the other. For a long time, we had wondered whether such a situation would arise. We have reached this position sooner than we expected and contrary to what our opponents expected. We must take care that this position does not slip from our hands even more easily.

Our opponents too entertain the thought that, just as it came easily to us, they can obtain it easily. We know how much envy and hatred they harbour toward us. Therefore, even if we do not gain more than what we now possess, I ask you to conduct yourselves honourably enough at least to retain what we have.

It is easy to make a name by speaking at a conference. Likewise, a person who runs a stall at a conference may make good money. But it is difficult to earn a good name through service. One must earn a good name among those whose good opinion matters. If everyone who arrives is allowed inside the pavilion, the whole town will celebrate, but a second conference will never be held.

Therefore, volunteers must discipline themselves according to the duty to which they have been assigned. Whether an elder brother, younger brother, uncle, or neighbour arrives, volunteers must see only the person who has come; they must not look upon him as elder brother, younger brother, or uncle.

At the same time, because every visitor must be treated simply as a person, one must not say, “Hey, come here like this; go there like that.” There must be kindness in the eyes and firmness as well. It is not easy to work within both these requirements. That is why this felicitation banquet and the presentation of a commemorative address are being held.

At every conference, groundless blame will arise. I know from experience that this has occurred at many conferences in many places. Some people levy false accusations; in some places, such things may actually have happened. Therefore, our volunteers must serve in a manner that brings honour and advancement to the movement.

The volunteers assembled here, the commander of the volunteer corps, and those who served as leaders of its smaller units have all worked extremely well. They are all worthy of praise. There remains a great deal of work for them to perform. I ask them to render still more service.

However strong the bricks used to build a house may be, mortar plastered upon the brick wall is what makes it firm. Likewise, only affection can give the movement greater strength and enable it to grow.

Even the Congress Party has endured for more than twenty or thirty years because of the affection its members have maintained toward one another. The relationship between Nehru and Pant too arose from affection.

If some faults or shortcomings arise in the movement, members must speak openly with one another, remove them, and cultivate affection.

Our movement is now growing very rapidly throughout the surrounding area. Among the volunteers here, only fifty are personally familiar to me. I have seen another fifty. I do not know the others well; in a little while, they too will become familiar to me. In this manner, our circle will grow larger day by day. As it grows, members must learn to associate with one another affectionately.

If you fail to cultivate such closeness, one day the public will say, “We do not know all these people.”

Therefore, I ask all of you to continue associating with the same affection you display now and to render abundant service in a manner that will bring honour to our movement and win the people’s praise.

---NOTES---
The source identifies itself as a summary rather than a verbatim transcript; English preserves that status.
“நேருவுக்கும் பந்துக்கும்” is rendered as Nehru and Pant; the proper name is contextually identifiable while the Tamil source remains unchanged.
